# font-demo

A portrait of the committed font tables, held long enough to be read. It draws
two lines at sixteen pixels and two at twelve, centred on the panel, the last
one in the Polish charset, so a person can tell a working font from a
scrambled one. Below them it lays out every glyph of the twelve pixel table,
wrapped to the panel's width, so no bitmap in the charset goes unseen. The
whole block is shown upright and then turned a quarter clockwise three times:
the text is always composed upright, and the packed frame is what turns, so
sideways text costs the font nothing.

It branches on the panel's depth: a one-bit panel receives the composed packed
rows directly, and a sixteen-bit panel receives each row expanded to RGB565 in
the two colours its line names, so each line can carry its own ink and paper.
Either way the composition is the same one-bit frame, which is the point of
mm.fonts: the text knows nothing about the panel, and colour is a property of
the expansion rather than of the font.

It names mm.fonts for the tables and the composition, mm.display for the
frame, and mm.mcu for the hold, and no board, SDK, or vendor.
